{"results":[{"id":"69cb7349f3c70800ebef7310","name":"Operation DualScript: Multi-Stage PowerShell Malware Targets Crypto","description":"Operation DualScript is a sophisticated multi-stage malware campaign targeting cryptocurrency and financial activities. It utilizes Windows Scheduled Tasks, VBScript launchers, and PowerShell execution to maintain persistence while minimizing disk artifacts. The attack operates through two parallel chains: a web-based PowerShell loader deploying a cryptocurrency clipboard hijacker, and a secondary chain executing the RetroRAT implant in memory. RetroRAT monitors user activity, captures keystrokes, and tracks interactions with financial services to harvest sensitive information. The malware employs various anti-analysis techniques and establishes a command-and-control channel for remote access and data exfiltration. This campaign highlights the growing abuse of trusted system utilities and in-memory execution techniques to evade traditional detection mechanisms.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-03-31T19:03:54.265000","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:01.875000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"","indicators":[{"id":4287905897,"indicator":"163c38bd7ff7dd27e88eaef1a7a4819f","type":"FileHash-MD5","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:02","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4287905898,"indicator":"173b27e7541427929da72ebf37c6db8e","type":"FileHash-MD5","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:02","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4287905899,"indicator":"1dc82fd02a0db3e338128b6f587d7122","type":"FileHash-MD5","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:02","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4287905900,"indicator":"243af69d85550232da45f5a30703a4a3","type":"FileHash-MD5","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:02","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4287905901,"indicator":"43cac07a501e7a717023e0fa8f6111e0","type":"FileHash-MD5","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:02","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4287905902,"indicator":"7546ada1e3144371724db209ba4c5f37","type":"FileHash-MD5","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:02","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4287905903,"indicator":"36c29d4238061ddfdd41735b4590c2239f019679","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:02","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4287905904,"indicator":"582eeb086e1e50f036a243e1ceb8837803c64ce4aa7208b3946c4b68b35fab10","type":"FileHash-SHA256","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:02","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":2919451260,"indicator":"anycourse.net","type":"domain","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:02","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4287905906,"indicator":"thewpiratebay.st","type":"domain","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:02","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4287905907,"indicator":"floatsdk.1cooldns.com","type":"hostname","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:02","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4287905908,"indicator":"info.1cooldns.com","type":"hostname","created":"2026-03-31T07:10:02","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null}],"tags":["cryptocurrency","retrorat","multi-stage","powershell","financial theft","clipboard hijacking","in-memory execution","evasion techniques"],"targeted_countries":["United States of America"],"malware_families":["RetroRAT"],"attack_ids":["T1053.005","T1113","T1056.001","T1115","T1071","T1057","T1041","T1059.001","T1562.001","T1027","T1056","T1059.005"],"references":["https:\/\/www.seqrite.com\/blog\/operation-dualscript-powershell-malware-retrorat-analysis\/"],"industries":["Finance"],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"69cbf2dc8db31bdbd9069344","name":"One Click Away: Inside a LinkedIn Phishing Attack","description":"A sophisticated phishing campaign targeting LinkedIn users has been identified. The attack uses fake LinkedIn message notifications to lure victims into clicking on malicious links. The emails closely mimic legitimate LinkedIn communications, including spoofed display names and formatting. Upon clicking, users are redirected to a convincing but fraudulent LinkedIn login page designed to steal credentials. The phishing page uses a deceptive domain name similar to 'LinkedIn' to further trick users. This campaign demonstrates the evolving tactics of cybercriminals in exploiting human trust and curiosity. The analysis emphasizes the importance of vigilance, source verification, and caution when interacting with seemingly routine notifications.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-03-31T18:41:40.249000","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:20.518000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"","indicators":[{"id":4288017777,"indicator":"inedin.digital","type":"domain","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:21","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4288017779,"indicator":"singletoncop.info","type":"domain","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:21","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4288017780,"indicator":"notifcation.inedin.digital","type":"hostname","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:21","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null}],"tags":["credential theft","social engineering","email spoofing","notification imitation","domain spoofing","fake login page","linkedin","phishing"],"targeted_countries":[],"malware_families":[],"attack_ids":["T1056.001","T1566.002","T1598.003","T1566.001","T1534","T1585.001","T1204.001","T1078.004"],"references":["https:\/\/cofense.com\/blog\/one-click-away-inside-a-linkedin-phishing-attack"],"industries":[],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"69cbf2e4685c6f31a7715a5f","name":"CrySome RAT : An Advanced Persistent .NET Remote Access Trojan","description":"CrySome is a sophisticated .NET-based remote access trojan designed for persistent command-and-control operations. It features advanced persistence mechanisms, including recovery partition abuse and offline registry modification, allowing it to survive system resets. The malware incorporates an aggressive defense evasion module, disabling security products and blocking updates. Key capabilities include command execution, file operations, surveillance, credential theft, and hidden virtual desktop control. CrySome's modular architecture and structured packet-based protocol enable a wide range of remote operations. Its emphasis on stealth, resilience, and comprehensive system control makes it a significant threat for long-term covert access to compromised environments.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-03-31T18:40:20.366000","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:28.748000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"","indicators":[{"id":4285300869,"indicator":"03898be29fb6c5464b28ae0239713b7b","type":"FileHash-MD5","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:29","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300870,"indicator":"d5e2eb1366ac6a691b5aaad8bec11727","type":"FileHash-MD5","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:29","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300871,"indicator":"a89158fe7d762dca8f136498a4120e3597933cab","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:29","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300872,"indicator":"b4070db8f451731ab768a530f6738cc1800a300b","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:29","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300873,"indicator":"f30f32937999abe4fa6e90234773e0528a4b2bd1d6de5323d59ac96cdb58f25d","type":"FileHash-SHA256","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:29","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300874,"indicator":"fa896cc8ce13c69f6306eff2a8698998b48b422784053df6bb078c17fe3f04c3","type":"FileHash-SHA256","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:29","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4289032094,"indicator":"61d065d0afd03bac6a42cb39d48115f66b9fb3ff","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:29","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300876,"indicator":"crysome.net","type":"domain","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:29","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null}],"tags":["rat",".net","stealth","c#","remote access","credential theft","defense evasion","crysome rat","hvnc","persistence","avkiller"],"targeted_countries":[],"malware_families":["CrySome RAT"],"attack_ids":["T1113","T1037","T1003","T1123","T1543","T1547","T1053","T1106","T1562","T1555","T1036","T1055","T1218","T1021","T1112","T1125","T1090","T1059","T1070","T1057","T1027","T1056","T1134","T1105"],"references":["https:\/\/www.cyfirma.com\/research\/crysome-rat-an-advanced-persistent-net-remote-access-trojan"],"industries":[],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"69cbf2e5f01a923f01d49ea8","name":"Phantom Footprints: Tracking GhostSocks Malware","description":"GhostSocks is an emerging threat that turns compromised devices into residential proxy nodes, enabling attackers to evade detection. Originally marketed on Russian underground forums as Malware-as-a-Service, it has gained popularity due to its partnership with Lumma Stealer. Written in GoLang, GhostSocks uses SOCKS5 proxy protocol and TLS encryption to blend malicious traffic into normal network activity. It also incorporates backdoor functionality for running arbitrary commands and deploying additional payloads. Darktrace observed an increase in GhostSocks activity, detecting it alongside Lumma Stealer in customer networks. The malware's versatility in converting devices into proxy nodes while enabling covert network access illustrates how threat actors maximize the value of compromised infrastructure.","author_name":"AlienVault","modified":"2026-03-31T18:38:43.912000","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:29.298000","revision":2,"tlp":"white","public":1,"adversary":"GhostSocks","indicators":[{"id":4285300558,"indicator":"ddd2994acd25bde5ac32a03f1cf30b41","type":"FileHash-MD5","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:30","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300559,"indicator":"10f928e00a1ed0181992a1e4771673566a02f4e3","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:30","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300560,"indicator":"3d9d7a7905e46a3e39a45405cb010c1baa735f9e","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:30","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300561,"indicator":"9b90c62299d4bed2e0752e2e1fc777ac50308534","type":"FileHash-SHA1","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:30","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300562,"indicator":"59312a8d6663c9a404d0b5aa96b70be3946592e5c5489366e04114b11a722fa1","type":"FileHash-SHA256","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:30","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300563,"indicator":"fab6525bf6e77249b74736cb74501a9491109dc7950688b3ae898354eb920413","type":"FileHash-SHA256","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:30","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4160589839,"indicator":"86.54.24.29","type":"IPv4","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:30","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":"2026-04-30T16:00:00","is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300566,"indicator":"retreaw.click","type":"domain","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:30","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300567,"indicator":"w2.bruggebogeyed.site","type":"hostname","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:30","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null},{"id":4285300568,"indicator":"www.lbfs.site","type":"hostname","created":"2026-03-31T16:14:30","content":"","title":"","description":"","expiration":null,"is_active":1,"role":null}],"tags":["c2 infrastructure","ghostsocks","evasion techniques","socks5","golang","lumma stealer","backdoor","tls encryption","residential proxy"],"targeted_countries":[],"malware_families":[],"attack_ids":["T1071","T1112","T1102","T1571","T1095","T1588","T1189"],"references":["https:\/\/www.darktrace.com\/blog\/phantom-footprints-tracking-ghostsocks-malware"],"industries":["Education"],"extract_source":[],"more_indicators":false},{"id":"69cbf2e593a215d1c46c988a","name":"New widespread EvilTokens kit: device code phishing as-a-service","description":"EvilTokens is a new Phishing-as-a-Service offering a turnkey Microsoft device code phishing kit. It enables attackers to harvest access and refresh tokens, granting unauthorized access to victims' Microsoft accounts. The kit supports post-compromise operations, allowing data exfiltration from various Microsoft services. EvilTokens has been rapidly adopted by cybercriminals since March 2026, impacting organizations globally. The service provides advanced capabilities for account takeover, including token conversion to Primary Refresh Tokens and browser cookies for persistent access. Phishing campaigns using EvilTokens target employees in finance, HR, logistics, and sales, primarily for Business Email Compromise attacks.","author_name"